/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.0" = "Microsoft discontinued Soundscape and released its source code. VoiceVista is licensed under the MIT License and continues to provide this service for people who are blind or have low vision.\nVoiceVista helps you stay aware of where you are and where you're going through unique audio guidance and callouts for roads, intersections, and places as you approach them.\nVoiceVista uses 3D sound to tell you what's around you. For example, if your destination is on your right, you will hear a sound from that direction. If a coffee shop is on your left, you will hear its callout from the left.\nPlace a beacon on your destination, and VoiceVista will keep you informed of its location and your surroundings along the way. Use VoiceVista with your wayfinding skills and your favorite navigation app to find your way to your destination.\nPut away your phone and walk. VoiceVista will help you stay aware of where you are by calling out roads, intersections, and landmarks as you approach them. You can adjust callouts in Settings.\nHold your phone flat in your hand, and use the buttons to locate roads and upcoming intersections, orient yourself to familiar landmarks, and explore landmarks ahead of you.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.1" = "VoiceVista offers features for a variety of situations and needs. Its value to you may evolve over time, so how you use it today may differ from how you use it in three months.\nPeople often think about apps in terms of the problem they solve. VoiceVista can certainly be used case by case when you have a specific need, such as keeping track of a destination, orienting yourself as you emerge from a metro station, getting your bearings when exiting a car, or learning the street names at the next intersection and how far away it is.\nHowever, VoiceVista's philosophy is to light up your world with sound. It is designed for use whenever you are out and about, providing ambient awareness of your surroundings by keeping you aware of the streets you are on, the direction you are heading, and the businesses you are passing.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.10" = "VoiceVista can determine your destination's location to within several meters, but not with pinpoint accuracy. When VoiceVista determines that you are close, you will hear a final callout that the destination is nearby, and the beacon audio will automatically mute.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.11" = "Yes. After VoiceVista automatically mutes the beacon, select Unmute Beacon to hear it again. However, because location accuracy may be limited to about 10 meters, we cannot guarantee the beacon's behavior when you are within a few meters of your destination.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.12" = "To accommodate different intersection layouts, VoiceVista describes intersections as road segments that depart from a common point.\nVoiceVista uses spatial audio to announce the road to the left, the road straight ahead, and the road to the right, in that order.\nIf the description begins with the road you are on instead of a road to the left, the intersection is a T where your road continues ahead and another road enters from the right.\nIf the description includes only a road to the left and one to the right, your road ends at a T ahead. This method also supports roads that change names at an intersection.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.13" = "VoiceVista is designed not to be too chatty. It uses OpenStreetMap as its map data source. OpenStreetMap is a community-developed map of the world that relies on contributors to enter and maintain its data. If VoiceVista does not announce a business or point of interest, it most likely has not yet been added or updated by the OpenStreetMap community.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.14" = "To keep the number of callouts from becoming overwhelming, VoiceVista does not automatically announce some categories, such as shops and restaurants, while you are traveling in a vehicle.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.15" = "VoiceVista keeps a list of recent callouts so you can revisit ones you missed. Tap the search field on the Surroundings tab, then find the missed callout in the Recent Callouts section at the bottom of the screen.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.16" = "VoiceVista requires iOS 15 or later and supports iPhone 6s and later models, including all iPhone SE models.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.17" = "VoiceVista supports spatial audio with dynamic head tracking on compatible AirPods. Sensors in connected AirPods tell VoiceVista which direction your head is facing.\nThis makes the audio experience feel more natural as you move. When using connected AirPods, you do not need to hold your phone for VoiceVista to work correctly.\nYou can use AirPods whenever you would normally use VoiceVista. They provide high-quality audio and a hands-free experience.\nTo connect your AirPods, open Head-Tracking Headphones in General Settings and follow the onscreen instructions. Pair your AirPods with your phone in Bluetooth settings before connecting them in VoiceVista.\nWhen media controls are enabled in VoiceVista, you can use the controls on your AirPods to mute or unmute the beacon, announce your current location, or repeat the last callout.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.18" = "You can access certain VoiceVista features with the media control buttons on your headphones.\nThis works with wired or Bluetooth headphones that have controls such as Play, Pause, Next, and Previous. Available buttons vary by model, so refer to the actions below.\nThis feature requires headphones that support Apple's media controls.\nYou can use headphone media controls while VoiceVista is in the foreground or background, even when your device is locked. They will not control VoiceVista while another app is playing audio, such as music, podcasts, or videos.\nThe following actions are available:\nPlay/Pause: Mute current callouts and, if a beacon is set, toggle its audio.\nNext: Announce your current location.\nPrevious: Repeat the last callout.\nSkip Forward: Turn automatic callouts on or off.\nSkip Backward: Announce what's around you.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.19" = "Battery life varies significantly depending on your iPhone's model and age. Keeping the screen on is one of the biggest drains on the battery, so keep the screen locked whenever possible.\nVoiceVista provides Sleep and Snooze modes to reduce its impact on your iPhone's battery. To reduce battery use further, force-close VoiceVista from the App Switcher when you are not using it.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.2" = "The Surroundings tab includes several options that provide information about where you are. Swipe up or down on each button to use rotor actions and get more specific information.\nBefore pressing a button on this tab, hold your phone flat with the screen facing the sky and the top pointing in the direction you are facing. The phone then acts like a compass, telling the app which direction you are facing.\nDouble-tap What's My Location to hear information about your current location, including the direction you are facing and nearby roads, intersections, and points of interest.\nSwipe down on What's My Location to save or share your current location.\nDouble-tap What's Around Me to hear about one item in each of the four directions around you: ahead, right, behind, and left.\nSwipe down on What's Around Me for options to hear nearby markers or list nearby places. Nearby Markers describes up to four of the markers closest to you. Use Explore Nearby to learn where familiar places are and orient yourself to your surroundings.\nDouble-tap What's Ahead of Me to hear about four items ahead of you. Swipe down on What's Ahead of Me for an option to list intersections ahead.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.20" = "To put VoiceVista into Sleep mode, select Sleep in the top-right corner of the Surroundings tab. VoiceVista will stop using Location Services and mobile data until you wake the app.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.21" = "To put VoiceVista into Snooze mode, select Sleep in the top-right corner of the Surroundings tab. Once VoiceVista is in Sleep mode, select Wake Up When I Leave. VoiceVista will enter a low-power state until you leave your current location.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.22" = "In our tests, Bluetooth headphones used about as much battery power as wired headphones, so connectivity should not be a significant factor when choosing headphones. Both earbuds and bone-conduction headphones are available in wired and wireless models.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.23" = "VoiceVista uses Location Services to determine your location. In our tests, it uses no more battery power than a typical map app. If you are concerned about battery use, try these tips:\n1. Turn off the screen whenever you are not interacting with the app.\n2. Close the app when you are not using it. VoiceVista uses Location Services continuously while running so that it always knows your location, even when you are not moving. Remember to restart the app when you resume your journey.\n3. Keep your phone warm in cold weather because batteries perform worse at lower temperatures.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.24" = "The amount of mobile data VoiceVista uses depends on how you use it. VoiceVista minimizes data use by caching map data as you move, so it does not need to download the same data again when you return to an area.\nConnect to Wi-Fi whenever possible to reduce mobile data use, particularly when downloading the app. When you are not using VoiceVista, select Sleep to put it to sleep or force-close the app.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.25" = "VoiceVista provides an ambient description of your surroundings to support exploration and wayfinding. Using spatial audio, it announces points of interest, parks, roads, and intersections from their respective directions as you walk.\nFor example, if you pass a shop on your right, you will hear its name from the right. As you approach an intersection, you will hear each road name from the direction in which that road extends, moving from your left, through the area ahead, to your right.\nVoiceVista plays a beacon from the direction of your destination, helping you travel on your own terms with greater awareness of your surroundings. It can also provide turn-by-turn directions to the beacon's destination when that option is enabled.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.26" = "VoiceVista is an awareness app that fills in details about your environment. Although it can provide turn-by-turn directions to the beacon's destination, you can also use it alongside other navigation apps for complementary information.\nStart your navigation app first. Then switch to VoiceVista and set a beacon on the same destination. Both apps will run at the same time: your navigation app will provide walking directions, while VoiceVista provides updates about points of interest, intersections, and your distance to the destination.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.27" = "VoiceVista provides several ways to control what you hear and when:\n1. Stop all audio immediately: Double-tap the screen with two fingers to stop all audio, including the current callout and beacon. Callouts resume automatically at the next intersection or point of interest, but the beacon does not. Select Unmute Beacon on the Surroundings tab to resume the beacon.\n2. Stop automatic callouts: When you are not traveling or have reached your destination, put VoiceVista into Snooze mode so it wakes when you leave, or into Sleep mode so it remains off until you wake it. You can also turn off Allow Automatic Callouts on the Settings tab.\n3. Stop the beacon: You may want distance updates without continuous beacon audio, or you may need the beacon only as you approach your destination. Use Mute Beacon or Unmute Beacon on the Surroundings tab to control when you hear it.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.28" = "No. While walking, you can put the phone in a bag, pocket, or any other convenient place.\nVoiceVista uses your direction of travel to determine which callouts to announce on your left and right.\nWhen you stop moving, VoiceVista does not know which direction you are facing. If the beacon is on, it becomes quieter until you start moving again.\nYou can take out the phone and use the location and exploration buttons at any time. Hold it flat with the screen facing the sky and the top pointing in the direction you are facing. In this position, VoiceVista uses the phone's compass to provide accurate spatial callouts. The beacon also returns to full volume.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.29" = "VoiceVista lets you customize key aspects of the callouts you hear while walking:\n1. Voice: Choose an available iOS voice and adjust its speaking rate under Settings > General Settings > Voice Settings. Download additional or enhanced voices under iOS Settings > Accessibility > Spoken Content > Voices.\n2. Distance units: Choose feet or meters under Settings > General Settings > Units of Measure.\n3. Markers: Mark personal places such as your home, office, or preferred grocery store. VoiceVista automatically calls out markers as you approach them, and Nearby Markers provides spatial callouts for markers around you. Your markers remain private.\n4. Callouts: VoiceVista automatically announces nearby businesses, bus stops, intersections, and other features using directional audio. Configure these under POI Settings, or turn off Allow Automatic Callouts when you want the app to remain silent.\nThe following VoiceOver settings can also affect VoiceVista:\n1. VoiceOver hints: When hints are enabled, VoiceOver provides more information about buttons on the Surroundings tab. Enable them under iOS Settings > Accessibility > VoiceOver > Verbosity > Speak Hints.\n2. Audio Ducking: VoiceVista works best with Audio Ducking turned off. Automatic callouts can be difficult to hear when VoiceOver uses audio ducking. Turn it off in VoiceOver settings or with the VoiceOver rotor.\n3. Touch ID or Face ID: Biometric unlocking gives you quick access to My Location, Around Me, and Ahead of Me while on the go. Set it up in the iOS Settings app.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.3" = "Markers are places you have saved. They may be places found in the app or new places you added yourself.\nTo save your current location as a marker, select the Markers tab and tap the button in the top-right corner. To save another location, select Explore Nearby on the Surroundings tab, then search for the place or select it from the nearby places.\nAfter finding the place, select it to open Location Details, then tap the button in the top-right corner.\nYou can mark any place or address, including things that may not appear on maps, such as building or park entrances, pedestrian push buttons, crossings, bridges, bus stops, or even your dog's favorite tree. You can then use them as references along your walk.\nVoiceVista automatically calls out marked places as you walk by or approach them. You can also use Nearby Markers on the Surroundings tab to hear spatial callouts for marked places around you.\nYou can set a beacon on any marked place. VoiceVista will play its familiar beacon, which you can control as usual.\nYou can customize a saved marker by changing its name or adding an annotation that will be called out with it.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.30" = "VoiceVista uses geographic data to provide map-based callouts. Its primary source is OpenStreetMap, a community-generated mapping platform. Organizations and individuals can use OpenStreetMap editing tools to improve the quality and detail of its information and document the accessibility of places.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.31" = "When there is no internet connection or VoiceVista's services are unavailable, VoiceVista enters offline mode. It continues to work, but some features may be limited.\nCallouts continue to work in areas with map data already stored on your device. You can also place a beacon or create a marker using places in Recent Callouts.\nVoiceVista tries to resume normal operation when your phone has a stable Wi-Fi or mobile data connection. If it does not, VoiceVista's services may be temporarily unavailable.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.32" = "Yes. You can create a BlindSquare backup of your places and import it into VoiceVista.\n1. Open BlindSquare and go to My Places.\n2. Tap the action button in the top-right corner.\n3. Email the exported file to yourself or save it in the Files app.\n• If you email the export, open the message in Mail, tap the attached JSON file, tap Share, and select VoiceVista.\n• If you save the export in Files, open VoiceVista and select Settings > Export and Import > Import BlindSquare Backup File.\nOnly the names and coordinates of BlindSquare markers are imported. Additional information, such as Alert Distance or the accuracy with which a marker was saved, cannot be imported.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.33" = "Motion & Fitness may be disabled for all apps. Open the Settings app, select Privacy & Security, then Motion & Fitness, and turn on Fitness Tracking.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.34" = "If you put your phone in your pocket and stop moving, the beacon becomes quieter because VoiceVista cannot tell which direction you are facing. Start walking again, or take out your phone and hold it flat.\nYou can set a beacon on any address. Search for the address from the Surroundings tab, select it in the results, then select Start Beacon on the Location Details screen. You can set beacons on businesses, places, points of interest, and residences that are not in OpenStreetMap.\nFind nearby bus stops by selecting the Public Transit filter in Explore Nearby.\nUse Mute Beacon on the Surroundings tab to turn off the beacon's rhythmic sound. You will continue to receive updates about your distance to the destination approximately every 50 meters.\nTo use VoiceVista without automatic callouts, turn off Allow Automatic Callouts on the Settings tab. If you are not using VoiceVista, select Sleep on the Surroundings tab to enter Sleep or Snooze mode.\nVoiceVista works best when you hold the phone flat with the screen facing the sky and the top pointing away from you.\nFor a bus route you use regularly, save your boarding and exit stops as markers. You can find them again on the Markers tab, set a beacon on them, and receive periodic updates as you approach your exit stop. Muting the rhythmic beacon does not stop distance updates.\nDouble-tap the screen with two fingers to silence active callouts.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.4" = "A route is a series of waypoints. VoiceVista informs you when you arrive at each waypoint, and the beacon automatically advances to the next one.\nYou may want to create a route in a familiar area to help you stay on track or use one to learn an unfamiliar route.\nTo create a route, open the Routes tab and select Add in the top-right corner. Give the route a name and an optional description, then add waypoints as you go or select them from your markers. You can rearrange the waypoints at any time by editing the route.\nAdvanced ways to create a route include using a navigation algorithm, importing a GPX file, or using Breadcrumb mode.\nTo edit a route, select it on the Routes tab, then select Edit Route Name or Edit Waypoints in the top-right corner. You can add and remove waypoints and edit the route's name and description.\nTo share a route, select it on the Routes tab and then select Share.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.5" = "You can set a beacon on any business, place, point of interest, address, or intersection. To view a location's details, search for a place or select a location from Explore Nearby or Markers.\nOn the Location Details screen, select Start Beacon. VoiceVista will return to the Surroundings tab and play a beacon from the direction of the selected place. The place's name, distance, and street address, when available, will appear on the Surroundings tab.\nA beacon helps you keep track of a nearby location by playing a sound from its direction. You can mute or unmute it on the Surroundings tab. VoiceVista also displays the location's distance and street address when known.\nUse a beacon to track a familiar landmark while exploring a new area or to track a destination while listening to information about your surroundings. It provides a continuous sound that indicates the direction of the destination relative to your current location. You can combine the beacon with your wayfinding skills and a navigation app to choose your own route.\nTo remove the current beacon, tap Stop Beacon on the Surroundings tab. To mute it, tap Mute Beacon.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.6" = "Think of the beacon as a lighthouse for your ears: it indicates where your destination is relative to your location, as the crow flies.\nLike a lighthouse, it does not tell you how to get there. You may need to make many navigation choices along the way, just as a sailboat makes strategic tacks to approach a lighthouse.\nThe beacon's continuous rhythmic sound comes from the direction of the destination, helping you remain aware of its location as you walk.\nWhen you walk directly toward the destination or point your phone toward it, you will hear a higher-pitched ring.\nThis ring helps you pinpoint the destination when the rhythmic sound is difficult to perceive in a loud environment. To find it, hold your phone flat and sweep it slowly. Turn your head in the same direction as the phone for the best spatial audio experience.\nThe lighthouse metaphor has several implications:\n1. There is no single correct direction to travel when using the beacon; you choose how to get there.\n2. The higher-pitched ring indicates only the direction of the destination, not how you should get there.\n3. If you generally know the route, you may want to mute the beacon for most of your trip and turn it on as you approach your destination.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.7" = "VoiceVista's beacon is a directional cue that indicates where your destination is relative to the direction you are facing. When VoiceVista is uncertain which direction you are facing, it lowers the beacon's volume.\nThis most often occurs when you stop moving after walking with the phone in a pocket or bag, such as while waiting to cross a street. The beacon becomes louder when you start moving again or hold the phone flat and point it in the direction you are facing.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.8" = "Yes. Search for the address, then select Save as Marker to save it or Start Beacon to begin tracking it immediately.";

/* No comment provided by engineer. */
"faq.a.9" = "To set a beacon on your home or another address, search for it from the Surroundings tab. On the Location Details screen, select Start Beacon.";

